# The primary flight display draws what HUD.cs draws.
#
# The facts name the elements the scene drew this frame; a vehicle on the link (SITL at
# tcp:127.0.0.1:5760) is what makes the readouts appear, since without one the display is a
# horizon at rest. `hud.missing` counts the doPaint() elements a live vehicle cannot show because
# mp_vehicle does not carry their value, and is 0: the last two, the flight path vector and the
# AOA scale, draw from AOA_SSA and the AOA_CRIT parameter. A regression that loses a value has to
# raise the count here. The two are not in `hud.drawn` against SITL's copter, which sends no
# AOA_SSA, so the C#'s displayAOASSA never turns on (HUD.cs:889-930); hud-health.gui asserts
# that. The health readouts along the bottom have their own script, hud-health.gui.

screen fly
window 1600x1200
settle 6

expect hud.drawn ~ sky-ground
expect hud.drawn ~ pitch-ladder
expect hud.drawn ~ heading-tape
expect hud.drawn ~ xtrack-bar
expect hud.drawn ~ rate-of-turn
expect hud.drawn ~ speed-tape
expect hud.drawn ~ altitude-tape
expect hud.drawn ~ vsi
expect hud.drawn ~ mode-and-waypoint
expect hud.drawn ~ battery
expect hud.drawn ~ gps
expect hud.drawn ~ armed-banner
expect hud.drawn ~ custom-items
expect hud.drawn ~ vibe
expect hud.drawn ~ ekf
expect hud.drawn ~ prearm
expect hud.missing 0
# "none", because a script cannot expect an empty value.
expect hud.missing.list none
